when it comes to the international  criminal court the philippine government  stand has always been consistent it says  it does not recognize icc's jurisdiction  over president rodrigo duterte and his  government after it has withdrawn as a  signatory of the rome statute in 2019  but the hague-based tribunal has  remained defiant and announced it will  open a full investigation into duterte's  so-called war on drugs which has killed  thousands of filipinos  the philippine government says at least  six thousand people have been killed in  police-led operations since 2016 but  rights groups believe that number could  go as high as thirty thousand  duterte's drug war is implemented across  the country many of the victims were  women and children and most of them come  from impoverished communities this is  why duterte's drug war has long been  seen as one against the poor  as long as uh  they give us at least a sliver of hope i  think it's not even just a sliver it's a  big window of opportunity as a chance  for the victims to have an alternative  if not parallel venue because their own  judicial system from their perspective  and perception have failed them  the investigation will also cover a  period when duterte was mayor of davao  city in the southern philippines where  prosecutors believe there has been a  similar repeated pattern of  extrajudicial killings  it's going to be a bloody fight  many believe duterte's rhetoric serves  as strong evidence their extrajudicial  killings in the philippines are  state-sponsored  even though it may take months or years  before witnesses are called to testify  or warrants may be issued families of  drug war victims already see this as a  moral victory and a step towards justice  they say president rodrigo duterte may  be popular domestically but he holds the  distinction of being the only filipino  leader to be tried and investigated in  the international criminal court for  crimes against humanity jamella  allendogen al jazeera manila
 

